    },
    {
      "key": "b608ae0c-0e53-486c-a793-3884e21791ea",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n**Circulate:** Teachers play a vital role in computer science education and supporting a collaborative and vibrant classroom environment. During online activities, the role of the teacher is primarily one of encouragement and support. Online lessons are meant to be student-centered, so teachers should avoid stepping in when students get stuck. Some ideas on how to do this are: \n\n - Utilize pair programming whenever possible during the activity.\n - Encourage students with questions/challenges to start by asking their partner.\n - Unanswered questions can be escalated to a nearby group, who might already know the solution.\n - Remind students to use the debugging process before you approach.\n - Have students describe the problem that they’re seeing. What is it supposed to do? What does it do? What does that tell you?\n - Remind frustrated students that frustration is a step on the path to learning, and that persistence will pay off.\n - If a student is still stuck after all of this, ask leading questions to get the student to spot an error on their own.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Discuss” slide\n\n**Discuss:** After providing students with end-of-class warnings, grab everyone's attention and get them to reflect on the experiences that they just had.\n\n - _Did anyone feel frustrated during any of the puzzles?_\n - _Did anyone notice the need to be persistent?_\n\n**Transition:** Have students take a moment to reflect on the lesson for themselves."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b608ae0c-0e53-486c-a793-3884e21791ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ef67755-63da-445a-bbcd-8c0411c54719"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3e9e528-64fb-4cd3-be64-3b2e8bafb938",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-list-alt\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Display**: Show “Reflect” slide\n\n**Reflect:**\n_Why is it important to program your blocks in a specific order?_\n",
        "name": "Reflection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3e9e528-64fb-4cd3-be64-3b2e8bafb938",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f7fc24cb-200e-499c-8d2e-6b06044dc00c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be9131ce-479b-4d65-be59-a23a11219ec2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Use these activities to enhance student learning. They can be used as outside of class activities or other enrichment.\r\n\r\n**Create Your Own**\r\n\r\nIn small groups, let students design their own mazes and challenge each other to write programs to solve them. For added fun, make life-size mazes with students as the pig and bird."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be9131ce-479b-4d65-be59-a23a11219ec2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66c833a1-5440-47f2-995c-139aba06f395"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "386d3b49-5d4b-4ae4-8b6b-01ad9cbf2420",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "###Bugs, Bugs & More Bugs (45-60 minutes)\n\n![](https://curriculum.code.org/media/uploads/elaSmall.png) ![](https://curriculum.code.org/media/uploads/mathSmall.png) **Computer Science + English Language Arts + Math**\n\n[Bugs, Bugs & More Bugs](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1TdvVXM9Tf8lv9L3rgDqMJFTe-iG0PjTpMtBb2f4pXDo/edit#)  is an optional activity aligned to Common Core ELA and Math standards, written by our teacher community. Students will practice debugging as they fix code, correct a math problem and proofread writing samples.\n\n*Standards Addressed*:\n- **CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.2.1.B:** Form and use frequently occurring irregular plural nouns (e.g., feet, children, teeth, mice, fish).\n\n- **CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.2.2.A:** Capitalize holidays, product names, and geographic names.\n\n- **CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.2.2.C:** Use an apostrophe to form contractions and frequently occurring possessives.\n\n- **CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.2.OA.B.2:** Fluently add and subtract within 20 using mental strategies.\n\n- **CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.2.NBT.A.4:** Compare two three-digit numbers based on meanings of the hundreds, tens, and ones digits, using >, =, and < symbols to record the results of comparisons.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "386d3b49-5d4b-4ae4-8b6b-01ad9cbf2420",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1624f575-8c20-4668-9521-879d8c8e2488"
      }
    }
  ],
